   177	## 3. Proposed: release format
   178	
   179	Proposed text for a new profile §15.9 and for the replacement of `V1-PROFILE.md:418-419` and `:425-426`. Every rule
   180	is **OWNER PROPOSAL 2026-10-19**; the source is given after each. Frozen at the 30 Oct freeze: R1–R6. Executed after
   181	the freeze, before the first public verifier release: the key, the ceremony, the bootstrap record, the rehearsal
   182	(A10.4.2).
   183	
   184	**R1. A release is one archive** [sources: A10:639-641; custody draft §2 "release archive"; profile §15.8].
   185	- The **release archive** is one file, `tessera-verifier-v1-<n>.parts` (production) or
   186	  `tessera-verifier-v1-TEST-<n>.parts` (TEST), where `<n>` is the canonical decimal release number.
   187	- Its bytes are the §15.8 part format: header grammar, sorted paths and verbatim blob bytes, all exactly as §15.8.
   188	  Every part is a tracked regular-file blob at one named **source commit**. Nothing is generated during assembly.
   189	- Required parts: `RELEASE.json` (R2); `trust.json`, the exact §15.5 trust-configuration bytes; `catalog.json`, the
   190	  exact frozen catalog bytes (item 7 fixes their format); `release_constants.py` (R2); the verifier source; the
   191	  pinned dependency lock (R6); the vendored SHA-1DC source with `SHA1DC-PIN.md`; the install and run scripts; the
   192	  relying-party text. No other part carries authority.
   193	- *Departure:* the custody draft names a `.tar`. The part format is used because one file set has exactly one byte
   194	  string (no modes, owners, timestamps or tar dialects). The existing assembler and its independent byte-agreement
   195	  check (`prototype/e2e/freeze.py:185-206`) let the review environment rebuild the archive from the source commit and
   196	  compare hashes, which C01's review-and-build step needs. Scripts run as `bash install.sh`, so no file mode is
   197	  needed. The archive is unpacked only after R3 succeeds.
   198	
   199	**R2. How the trust configuration and catalog bind** [sources: profile §5 `:410-417`; A10:639-641;
   200	`build_release.py`; `release_auth.provision`].
   201	- `RELEASE.json` is one JCS object with string values only and exactly these keys: `format`
   202	  (`"tessera-v1-release/1"`), `namespace` (`"PRODUCTION"` or `"TEST"`), `release` (= `<n>`), `profile`
   203	  (`"tessera-v1-strict"`), `source_commit` (40 lowercase hex), `trust_config_sha256`, `trust_config_version`,
   204	  `catalog_sha256`, `freeze_record_sha256`, `release_key_successor` (a v4 fingerprint, 40 uppercase hex, or null).
   205	- **Binding equalities**, checked before signing and again by `install.sh`; any failure means no installation:
   206	  SHA-256(`trust.json`) = `trust_config_sha256` = `release_constants.TRUST_SHA256`; SHA-256(`catalog.json`) =
   207	  `catalog_sha256` = `release_constants.CATALOG_SHA256`, and `CATALOG_BYTES` equals `catalog.json`; the version
   208	  inside `trust.json` = `trust_config_version`. Under `PRODUCTION`, `catalog_sha256` equals the catalog hash in the
   209	  freeze record whose SHA-256 is `freeze_record_sha256`.
   210	- At run time the existing rule is unchanged: a `--trust` file whose hash is not `TRUST_SHA256`, or that fails §15.5,
   211	  is `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, with no verdict.
   212	- Each new release's `trust.json` passes the release checker (§5 release invariants, EA-12) against the previous
   213	  release's `trust.json`.
   214	- *Departure:* `build_release.py` today writes `UNICODE_MODULE_SHA256` and `SHA1DC_AVAILABLE` from the build machine
   215	  into `release_constants.py`, and `verify.py` compares the running interpreter's `unicodedata` file hash with it.
   216	  Those are facts about one installation. They move to install time (as `install.sh` already does with
   217	  `sha1dc_build.json`), so that the archive's bytes do not depend on the build machine.
   218	
   219	**R3. The release signature** [sources: custody draft §3 `:63-77`; profile §8 rule O; profile §15.4].
   220	- The signature is a **detached, binary (unarmored) OpenPGP v4 signature of type 0x00** over the exact archive
   221	  bytes, file `<archive>.sig`. Algorithm ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256. It satisfies rule O's grammar and policy
   222	  (section 8), as the git `gpgsig` does after dearmoring (§15.4). GnuPG's output meets this (§8, "GnuPG's output …
   223	  satisfies it").
   224	- The release key is **one v4 ECDSA P-256 primary key with no subkeys**. Its identity is its full v4 fingerprint.
   225	  A signature by any other key, or by a subkey, does not authenticate a release.
   226	- The signature covers the whole archive: the code, the lock, `trust.json`, `catalog.json` and `RELEASE.json`. The
   227	  key never signs a hash written as text (custody draft §3).
   228	- **Relying-party acceptance.** Accept iff the pinned `gpgv` exits 0 on `<archive>.sig <archive>` with a keyring that
   229	  holds **only** the production key whose full fingerprint was obtained by R5. The signing session also checks the
   230	  new signature against rule O before it leaves the session.
   231	- *Departure:* the TEST stand-in (`tessera-v1-test-release/1`) signs only the JCS of `{catalog, catalog_sha256,
   232	  format, trust_config_sha256}` with a raw r‖s value and names the signer by SPKI hash. It does not cover the
   233	  verifier code, so a substituted verifier with a genuine record would pass. It also cannot be produced by the
   234	  YubiKey's OpenPGP applet through GnuPG, which the custody draft uses. It stays only as the §3.5 instrument
   235	  interface for the conformance and differential harnesses, and it is never a release.
   236	
   237	**R4. TEST and production** [sources: A10.4.2 row 36 (A10:772), A10:644-660; `release_auth.py`;
   238	`test_release_auth.py:135-143`].
   239	- Every release before the first public verifier release is a **TEST release**: `namespace` `"TEST"`, a `TEST` file
   240	  name, signed by a TEST release key in the R3 format. TEST keys are throwaway v4 P-256 keys and may live on the
   241	  operational computer (as `prototype/e2e/repo.py` does for the TEST repository key), because they authenticate
   242	  nothing. A TEST public key is never published as the release key, never named in the freeze record, the
   243	  bootstrap record or relying-party text, and never named in any `release_key_successor`.
   244	- The production key signs only `namespace` `"PRODUCTION"`. A TEST key signs only `"TEST"`. The signing session
   245	  checks this before signing.
   246	- **Production refusal is by key, not by label.** A TEST signature fails R3 because the production keyring holds
   247	  only the production key. The `TEST` label prevents honest mistakes. It is not a security check, because an
   248	  attacker can remove it (XP-02).
   249	- A `PRODUCTION` archive's `release_constants.py` sets `PRODUCTION = True`. With it, the verifier refuses
   250	  `--test-release`, `--test-release-key` and any `TESSERA_V1_TEST_*` variable with `TEST_AUTHORITY_REFUSED` and no
   251	  verdict, whatever the command line says. *Departure:* today production mode is a CLI flag
   252	  (`verify.py:307`, `release_auth.authenticate(..., production)`), so an invocation without it accepts TEST
   253	  provisioning. The refusal logic itself is reused unchanged.
   254	- **Rehearsal (A10 row 36).** Before the first public release: an otherwise correct archive signed only by a TEST key
   255	  is refused by the R3/R5 acquisition procedure, including when the TEST public key is offered as the key; and the
   256	  installed production verifier refuses the TEST interface. The second half is
   257	  `test_release_auth.py:135-143`; the first half is new.
   258	
   259	**R5. Out-of-band authentication of the production key** [sources: A10:668-674 (bootstrap record); custody draft
   260	§8 "Public-key bootstrap" and §10; contract review XP-02 (`docs/reviews/exit-pages/CODEX-REVIEW.md:9-13`);
   261	profile §5 `:418-419`].
   262	- The relying party's root of trust is the production key's **full v4 fingerprint**, obtained **before and apart
   263	  from** any release. A key or fingerprint that arrives with a release, on the same page as a release, or from the
   264	  operational computer, the repository, DNS or the issuing AWS account authenticates nothing.
   265	- The fingerprint is recorded in the dated **first-production-release bootstrap record**. That record names the
   266	  freeze record (by hash) and the production key, and is published and OpenTimestamps-stamped. Publication and
   267	  stamping give a dated public record. They are not authentication.
   268	- **Delivery channel (owner proposal; the author names the final one, custody draft §8 step 5).** For V1's one
   269	  tester (4 Dec): the author gives the full fingerprint directly, in person or by a voice call to a number the tester
   270	  already knows, and the tester compares all 40 hex characters. The author can add a second channel for others. If
   271	  no independent channel is named, the relying-party text states authenticated first acquisition as an assumption,
   272	  as A10.4.2 does ("does not protect a user who … acquires a substituted initial key").
   273	- **Rotation.** A new release key is introduced only by a production release, signed by the current key, whose
   274	  `release_key_successor` names it. The next release is then signed by the successor. (Profile §5 already says
   275	  this.)
   276	- **Loss or compromise.** V1 has no assured recovery. New releases stop. A replacement key needs a new out-of-band
   277	  delivery and a new dated bootstrap record. Genuine old releases stay available for existing receipts (custody
   278	  draft §10).
   279	- **Replacement for `V1-PROFILE.md:418-419`:** "Bootstrap: the production release key's full fingerprint is
   280	  delivered out of band (§15.9 R5) and recorded in the dated first-production-release bootstrap record (A10.4.2).
   281	  The freeze record does not contain it. A new release key is introduced only by a release signed by the current
   282	  one."
   283	
   284	**R6. Dependency pinning** [sources: profile §9, §14.2 item 15; `packaging/install.sh`; `SHA1DC-PIN.md`].
   285	- The archive carries a lock with exact versions **and SHA-256 hashes** for every Python package, installed with
   286	  hash checking required. Wheels are retrieved at install time and are not distributed. *Departure:*
   287	  `requirements.txt` pins `cryptography==50.0.2` without a hash.
   288	- Python 3.14 with Unicode 16.0.0 tables is checked at install and at start (as today). SHA-1DC is built from the
   289	  vendored, digest-checked source (as today).
   290	- The relying party's `gpgv` version and the OpenPGP standard it implements are entries in the dependency manifest
   291	  (item 15). Verification is offline after installation; installation is not.
   292	
   293	**What the TEST pipeline already does that R1–R6 keep:** the trust-hash refusal, the catalog-hash refusal, the
   294	release checker, `TEST_AUTHORITY_REFUSED`, the §15.8 assembler and byte-agreement check, the SHA-1DC pin and the
   295	install-time checks.
   296	
   297	## 4. The two hardest open questions in the release format
   298	
   299	1. **Which independent channel delivers the production fingerprint?** Every other rule reduces to "check against
   300	   the authenticated key", and XP-02 rules out the easy answers. The operational computer, the repository, DNS and a
   301	   pair of web pages are all excluded, and so is anything that travels with a release. Voice or in-person delivery
   302	   works for one tester and does not scale. If the author names no channel, every relying party except a
   303	   hand-delivered one relies on an assumption, and G1–G10 then hold only conditionally (contract §2, "the checker's
   304	   verifier release is authentic"). Only the author can supply this channel. It is due before the first public
   305	   release, not the freeze, but the relying-party text frozen on 30 Oct must already say which case applies.
   306	2. **What does the frozen format bind when the production verifier code does not exist yet?** The exit opens
   307	   Phase 1a "for V1-profile code only" (A9:218). The contract says there is only one V1 verifier, and today that is
   308	   the instrument `prototype/indep-verifier/`. R1 freezes the container, the manifest, the bindings and the
   309	   signature, but not the code inventory. Two things must be decided by 30 Oct: (a) whether the first public
   310	   release ships the instrument verifier (promoted out of `prototype/`) or new Phase 1a code; and (b) which parts
   311	   are mandatory, so that the review-and-build check (C01) has a fixed list to compare. If (a) is "new code", the
   312	   rehearsal and the e2e TEST releases test a different program from the one that is shipped.
   313	
