     1	# E9 read packet: the 30 Oct V1 exit
     2	
     3	> **For the author.** Owner draft, 2026-10-21 (Claude Opus 5.5), revised after Codex review E9-01 to E9-14
     4	> ([disposition](reviews/e9-packet/DISPOSITION.md)). A9.2: "The author's E9 read is accept or reject per row"
     5	> ([A9:222](phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md#L222)). One ruling (row 2), accept or reject on the rest, 30–45 minutes.
     6	> One outside read is required (row 22, 8 minutes). Links are for checking only.
     7	
     8	## What the V1 exit establishes
     9	
    10	**The claim.** A user holds a V1 receipt, an authenticated verifier release and its trust file. Without Tessera,
    11	the user can check four things:
    12	
    13	1. The receipt binds exact content bytes.
    14	2. The verifier checks signatures by both keys named in the receipt, over frames that bind those bytes.
    15	3. DNSSEC evidence and a signed git commit name those keys for `wamason.com`.
    16	4. The bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block.
    17	
    18	This establishes signatures by Tessera's actual service keys only if the authority records named those actual keys.
    19	Capture of both authority records can defeat that assurance (row 2). Every item depends on the registered
    20	assumptions about cryptography, key use, Bitcoin history and the verifier release.
    21	
    22	**The attacker.** The attacker controls the network. Changing the content, or the frames and signatures committed
    23	by the receipt anchor (`A_sig`), prevents acceptance unless the registered security assumptions fail. Some changes
    24	outside that commitment remain valid: for example, another conforming possession signature over the same manifest.
    25	Its own keys get no VALID verdict unless it captures the authority records. If it captures **both** the DNS record
    26	and the git repository, its new receipts under its own keys **pass under CUT** (row 2); **under KEEP**, an authentic
    27	verifier release refuses them, because its listing does not name those keys. Either way, receipts that the honest
    28	service signed stay protected while the service keys stay secret and unmisused. Capture of the issuing AWS account
    29	lets the attacker make receipts that pass.
    30	
    31	**The boundaries.** V1 does not check who sent the bytes, what they mean, revocation, or past key or algorithm
    32	safety. It gives no lower time bound. T is a Bitcoin header time, not real time; a real-time bound U holds only under
    33	A-UTC, which the verifier does not check. V1 detects no capture and checks no publication. The same content can have
    34	more than one valid receipt.
    35	
    36	**What the exit is.** The packet reports the completed checks and names remaining work. Your ACCEPT answers
    37	complete the author's E9 judgments. The owner must then close the remaining freeze prerequisites, including the
    38	provisional release-checker choice, and reconcile the final objects and record. The signed opening commit follows
    39	those checks. It permits V1-profile code only and discharges nothing in the original Band 0 gate.
    40	
    41	## How the rows were filtered
    42	
    43	A9.2's closure rule sends you a finding only if it is a concrete counterexample to C1–C7, or shows that a C-row lacks
    44	its assigned evidence. The owner sorted all 93 findings since 19 Oct
    45	([P8](reviews/p8-falsification/DISPOSITION.md), [E18](reviews/e18-signoff/DISPOSITION.md),
    46	[E6a](reviews/e6a/DISPOSITION.md), [exit pages](reviews/exit-pages/DISPOSITION.md),
