     2	
     3	> **OWNER DECISION, 2026-10-19** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance). GATE action that the registered text already
     4	> provides: A10 row 33 makes the listing the second item cut and states what applies when it is cut. No amendment.
     5	> The author adopted option (e) on 2026-10-18, including row 33. He can override this cut until the freeze assembly
     6	> (26 Oct, `docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`); after that, putting the listing back means a new spec object.
     7	
     8	> **CORRECTION, 2026-10-21 (owner).** This cut is **not** authorized by the registered text as claimed below. A10 row 33
     9	> amends A9.2's cut order, and that order applies only "if the 30 Oct exit slips" (A9.2). The exit had not slipped;
    10	> the cut rested on a forecast of about one day of slack. It reverses part of option (e), which the author adopted
    11	> on 2026-10-18, so it is the **author's ruling**, put to him in `docs/E9-READ-PACKET.md`. Until he rules, the freeze
    12	> is assembled without the listing and nothing is final before the opening commit. The owner still recommends the
    13	> cut, on the merits in "Why" items 2 and 3; item 1 (schedule) is weaker since the freeze was assembled on
    14	> 2026-10-21, not 2026-10-26. The original text is left below unchanged.
    15	
    16	> **AUTHOR RULING, 2026-10-23 (E9 row 2): CUT.** The author's rationale (recorded in full in the rulings file): keep
    17	> V1's complexity manageable, accept the stated dual-authority-capture risk for early receipts given low expected
    18	> initial use, and preserve the option to protect future issuance. It is not the owner's argument adopted
    19	> wholesale; parts of that argument were qualified in review. It is an author scope decision that narrows option (e), not an invocation of the cut order, and it does not retroactively authorize the 2026-10-19 cut
    20	> (`docs/reviews/e9-packet/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md`). A later version may add a listing for future issuance; it cannot
    21	> cover receipts issued before it.
    22	
    23	## What is cut
    24	
    25	A10.4.1 and the rows that take effect only under option (e) with it: 25–32 and 37–39. These are the `service_keys`
    26	trust-configuration member, its classifier and reason codes, the listing pre-commitment, the C0/C1 bootstrap, and
    27	the listing-evidence package that relying parties would have to keep outside the bundle.
    28	
    29	## What applies instead (row 33)
    30	
    31	A10.4 option (a): honest signed material stays protected under authority capture (rows 15–16; contract G10). **V1
    32	gives no protection against new receipts under a captor's own keys** when both authority channels are captured, and
    33	the relying-party text and the freeze record say so. Release-key custody (A10.4.2) is unchanged: the cut does not
    34	cure a failure of release-key independence, and the custody gate at the first public verifier release still holds.
    35	
    36	## Why
    37	
    38	1. **Schedule.** The 30 Oct exit audit (`docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`) puts the listing on the critical path, with about
    39	   one working day of slack. Cutting it saves about 4 days (owner estimate). The first cut item, the PQ hybrid, is
    40	   already registered and would save almost nothing.
    41	2. **What it buys is narrow.** It protects only relying parties whose verifier release is authentic. After review
    42	   r4 its protection against a later release-key theft needs independently authenticated Bitcoin history. It moves
    43	   trust from two credentials (DNS and repository key) to a third (the release key), and all three have the same
    44	   custodian.
    45	3. **What it costs relying parties.** A listing-evidence package outside the receipt bundle that holders must keep
    46	   (contract review VC-02). This is the one mechanism added purely for a decades-later adjudicator
    47	   (`docs/decisions/2026-10-19-horizon-cost.md`), and it is the one the author asked about and got no clear answer to.
    48	
    49	## What is lost, honestly
    50	
    51	- Production receipts issued before any later listing can never have its protection. A listing added in a later
    52	  version protects only receipts after it.
    53	- The horizon analysis recommended keeping the listing at its signed scope, on that ground. The owner weighs the
    54	  schedule and the relying-party burden above it for V1.
    55	
    56	## Registered as a candidate, not an obligation
    57	
    58	A key listing with a chain-order pre-commitment, or a witnessed log of key assertions, is a candidate for a later
    59	version. Neither creates an obligation.
    60	
    61	## Author ruling, 2026-10-23
    62	
    63	The author chose **CUT** for E9 Row 2, accepting the dual-authority-capture
    64	limitation to keep V1's complexity manageable, with low expected initial
    65	use and the option to protect future issuance. This is an author narrowing
    66	of the previously adopted scope, independently of the deadline; it does not
    67	retroactively authorize the owner's original cut. The full ruling and
    68	upgrade boundary are recorded in
    69	[`AUTHOR-RULINGS.md`](../reviews/e9-packet/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md).

