docs/reviews/p8-falsification/DISPOSITION.md:17:| P8F-02 accepted number spellings crash B | PROOF-GAP (implementation) | **Implementation repaired** to match E§1.2. B's `_decimal` no longer calls `int()` on an unbounded exponent: it strips leading zeros and clamps exponents of more than 18 significant digits to ±10^18. That preserves every verdict, because a literal is at most 2^24 bytes, so no nonzero literal with such an exponent is a finite nonzero double (`number()` rejects it before comparing) and zero is decided first. **Owner-found, same class:** implementation A (`gen.py`) crashed on the same inputs, and its direct-decision bound (\|exponent\| > 100000) wrongly rejected value-preserving literals such as `1` + 200000 zeros + `e-200000`; A now uses the same 18-digit bound. No text change. | `formal/p8/vectors/check.py` `_decimal`; `formal/p8/vectors/gen.py` `number_value` | `witness` rerun below; `test_check.py` `test_oversized_exponents` (A and B); I regressions in `test_parsers.py`. |
docs/reviews/p8-falsification/DISPOSITION.md:18:| P8F-03 first-failing-category order not implemented | PROOF-GAP (implementation) | **Implementation repaired** to match E§1.4 (JSON_ENCODING over the whole input before JSON_SYNTAX). B and A now decode strict UTF-8 first, then reject a leading U+FEFF as `JSON_SYNTAX`. XC-19 (BOM + `{}`) is still `JSON_SYNTAX`. No text change. | `check.py` `parse_text`; `gen.py` `_parse_tree` | `witness` rerun below; the review's campaign rerun: disagreement classes `[]` (was 24 `mutant-ingest` JSON_SYNTAX/JSON_ENCODING), `campaign-rerun.log` here. |
docs/reviews/p8-falsification/DISPOSITION.md:19:| P8F-04 standalone payload exceeds the size domain | PROOF-GAP (implementation) | **Implementation repaired**: B's `payload_value` and A's `decode_payload` reject more than 2^24 bytes with `LENGTH_CAP` before parsing, as the frame's `payload_length` check already does. **CLARIFY** in E§1.4 stating this for `payload(bytes)`. `LENGTH_CAP` is an existing code, and every frame-path outcome is unchanged. I's generic `parse_json` is left uncapped, as the report allows: I has no standalone payload entry point, and its frame reader enforces the cap. | `check.py` `payload_value`; `gen.py` `decode_payload`; ENCODING-V1 §1.4 | `oversize` rerun below; `test_standalone_payload_cap` (A and B). |
