    11	## When to use
    12	
    13	Use for a read-only review of Tessera ProVerif/TLA+ artifacts, S-P3, or `LEDGER.md` capstone claims. Do not use it to silently amend adopted artifacts, decide unresolved policy questions, or convert a scratch probe, companion, or viability run into discharge.
    14	
    15	## Inputs and context
    16	
    17	1. Establish the exact artifact, registered property/amendment, current adoption/status record, and requested decision.
    18	2. Read the relevant model and its header, predictions/results, theory library, and any routed review record. Amendments outrank trackers.
    19	3. Recheck live branch/status and preserve unrelated pre-existing work as user-owned.
    20	
    21	## Procedure
    22	
    23	1. Explain local terms before testing conclusions. Translate the review as: claim -> adversary -> load-bearing check -> assumptions -> non-claims -> validation evidence.
    24	2. State the model boundary precisely: which keys/channels are compromised, which signed bytes or frames are reused, and which attacks are outside scope.
    25	3. Run the registered command when practical, preserving tool version and exact result polarity. For S-P3 Q2 use:
    26	   ```sh
    27	   proverif -lib formal/suite/lib/tessera_theory.pvl formal/suite/s-p3/proverif/sp3_q2_degraded_compromised.pv
    28	   ```
    29	4. For `LEDGER.md`, read the full relevant contract and combined-consequences table, not a single section. Translate each producer/consumer relation into which component supplies information and which relies on it.
    30	5. For every claimed cross-model join, identify the registered producer query and a broken companion whose mutation makes the consuming query fail. If severing the producer does not affect the consumer's registered query, call the relation complementary or capstone-internal rather than cross-model.
    31	6. Check relation scope structurally. A key/signature transplant query cannot establish exact signing-to-presented-bytes binding: C-Q8 must inspect accepted `(key, signature, presented bytes)` triples. Require C-Q9 separately for possession over the accepted manifest, and an acceptance/execution identifier for C-Q1 completeness.
    32	7. For composition, ask whether all checks run on one acceptance path. A wrapper can attest that it saw a bundle, but cannot repair an original attestation that was already forgeable; separate envelope validity, standing, authorship, and adjudicator judgment.
    33	8. Separate registered evidence, scratch-probe evidence, header fidelity, property coverage, symbolic proof, concrete cryptographic/security claims, cross-formalism claims, and implementation conformance in the conclusion.
    34	
    35	## Efficiency plan
    36	
    37	- Start with the requested artifact plus its adjacent predictions/results and governing amendment; for a capstone review, also read the complete ledger contract and combined-consequences table.
    38	- Cache the claim/adversary/boundary table while comparing the model, header, and review record.
    39	- Stop and ask for a decision when an apparent inconsistency is a policy choice or the adoption state is unclear.
